  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nThe central idea is to use a multi-grid simulated annealing (SA) optimization approach with adaptive resolution to navigate the complex solution space of the minimum overlap problem. This method combines:\n1. Resolution adaptation: Starting from coarse grids (10-250 points) to escape local optima, then upscaling to higher resolutions (500-1000 points) for refined solutions\n2. Simulated annealing: Probabilistic search that balances exploration and exploitation, avoiding stagnation at local minima\n3. Intensity modulation: Dynamically controlled search intensity to prioritize promising regions while preventing oversampling\n4. Update blending: Combinatorial merging of solutions from different grid scales for information transfer\n\nThe key advantage is computational efficiency through grid transitions and SA restarts. Unlike previous methods using continuous optimization with projected gradients, this approach employs randomized discrete changes with probabilistic acceptance, potentially discovering solutions not reachable through gradient-based techniques.\n\nMathematical Specification\n- **Representation**: Step functions as piecewise constant functions defined on grid points\n- **Variables**: h \u2208 [0,1]\u207f, normalized such that \u2211h\u1d62 * dx = 1 (dx = 2/n, n = resolution)\n- **Objective**: Minimize C\u2085 = max\u2096 [\u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx] \n  - Computed via: (2/n) * max(mode='full' cross-correlation of h and 1-h)\n- **Constraints**: \n  1. 0 \u2264 h\u1d62 \u2264 1 for all i\n  2. \u2211h\u1d62 = n/2 (exactly) (impacts grid size through dx)\n- **Implementation**: \n  - SA's objective function approximates gradient through finite differences\n  - Grid project_feasible() adjusts vectors to satisfy constraints\n  - Multi-grid transfer via linear interpolation maintaining function continuity\n\nImplementation Procedure\n1. **Parent Validation**\n   - Check input parent matches grid resolution\n   - Initialize custom distributions based on grid scale\n\n2. **Resolution Management**\n   - Designate grid sizes: coarse (n_init \u2208 [10,50]), medium (n_mid \u2208 [50,250]), dense (n_dens \u2208 [500,1000])\n   - Initialize based on smallest grid then transfer solutions\n\n3. **Simulated Annealing** (core optimization)\n   - Temperature control: T = T_exp * (1 / (1 + i / cooling_rate))\n   - Perturbation: Randomly select two indices, transfer mass value\u00b10.02 (or scaled by function variance)\n   - Acceptance:give_low = good; give_high = exp(-\u0394C/T)\n   - Update cooling rate: T_next = T * (c_cool if no_improve > 10 else 0.99)\n   - Early termination: Exceed 1000 SA iterations or 3 grid reschedules\n\n4. **Multi-grid Integration**\n   - Coarse-to-medium transfer: $\\hat{h}_{medium} = interpolator(h_{coarse})$\n   - Medium-to-dense: Apply SA refinement (dSq = high intensity)\n   - Bi-grid comparison: Accept new solution if C\u2085 better\n\n5. **Problem Shape Optimization**\n   - Special case for low resolutions (n=5): Direct analytical solution\n   - For n=10-20: Initialize with beam patterns (sinc-like structures)\n   - For n>20: Use momentum-based upsampling from nearest grid\n\nSearch Choices and Variations\n- **Randomized Initial Schemes**:\n  1. Flat distribution: h\u1d62 \u2248 0.5 for all i\n  2. Wavelet distribution: Gaussian ridges with width 1+f\u2082/f\u2083\n  3. Adaptive random: Poisson-distributed bases with FDR filtering\n- **Local Search Swaps**:\n  - Phase 1: Basic SA as main\n  - Phase 2: Insert 10% swap chance between grids\n  - Phase 3: Add penalty for solutions with identical C\u2085 values\n- **Advanced Variations**:\n  1. Genetic crossover at high temperatures (~80% acceptance)\n  2. Simulated tempering with T_step=0.1\n  3. Coarsening-based restart after 300 iterations\n\nCompute Budget and Robustness\n- **Estimates**:\n  - n=100 grid: ~5 seconds/SA = 300-5000 iterations budget\n  - n=500 grid: ~15 seconds/SA = 1000-50000 iterations budget\n- **Time Checkpoints**: Verify grid timing at 25%, 50%, 75% computed budget\n- **Fallback**: \n  - If any grid exceeds allocated time: Return best_from_previous_grid solution\n  - If gradient unstable: Fall back to grid-independent annealing\n- **Failure Handling**:\n  - NaN values: Trigger coarser grid restart\n  - Out-of-bounds: Increase regularization by 50%\n  - Inconsistent dimensions: Use closest available grid\n\nValidation and Return Contract\n1. Final re-evaluation:\n   - Compute C\u2085 with same np.correlate() method\n   - Check integral via sum(h)*dx == 1 (tolerance 1e-15)\n   - Ensure values clamped w/$project\\_feasible()$\n2. One validity check:\n   - Manual sanity: All h[i] should be non-extreme, wavelet-like\n   - Numerical safeguard: Clip input values between [0,1] before processing\n   - Output formatting: Return (h, C\u2085, n_points) strictly as numpy arrays\n\nMultistage Execution:\n- Phase 1: Coarse grid exploration (n=20) \u2192 best, C\u2085_value\n- Phase 2: Medium grid optimization (n=100) \u2192 best, C\u2085_value\n- Phase 3: Sensor grid application (n=500) \u2192 final solution\n- Final polish: Check all constraints and normalization",
